course: dictionary
lesson: 5225
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. Jelly can mean a dessert, a spread for bread, a soft wobbly substance, a jellyfish, or weak legs. The words around it show you which one the writer means.
explain: «Peanut butter and jelly» is a sandwich, so jelly must be the sweet spread that goes on bread. At a party, «jelly and ice cream» means the wobbly dessert. The other words in the phrase are the clues, so read them first.
explain: «A female common frog lays eggs in a jelly clump» and «frogspawn wobbles in the pond like jelly» both describe something soft and wobbly. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «a soft, wobbly clump» make sense? Then it is right. Phrases can also be tricky: «legs turned to jelly» does not mean legs made of food. It means weak, shaky legs from fear or tiredness.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one is right.
fact: «Jelly» can mean a sweet, wobbly dessert set in a mould (UK, Australia, NZ, Ireland).
fact: «Jelly» can mean a sweet spread for bread, like jam but clear (US, Canada), as in «peanut butter and jelly».
fact: «Legs like jelly» means weak, shaky legs from fear or tiredness. Informally, «jelly» can also mean a jellyfish.
exercise: In «peanut butter and jelly», what is jelly? | Weak, shaky legs | A wobbly dessert set in a mould | A jellyfish in the sea | *A sweet spread made from fruit juice and sugar
exercise: «Her legs turned to jelly after the long run.» What does jelly mean here? | A soft spread for toast | A jellyfish in the sea | A sweet dessert made from fruit juice | *Weak and shaky from tiredness
exercise: «A clump of frogspawn wobbles in the pond like jelly.» What does jelly mean here? | *Any soft, wobbly substance | A sweet dessert to eat with ice cream | A jellyfish swimming in the pond | A sticky sweet spread
test: «Jelly and ice cream» is a party treat in the UK. What does jelly mean here? | Weak, shaky legs | *A sweet, wobbly dessert set in a mould | A jellyfish | A sweet spread for bread
test: «A female common frog lays eggs in a jelly clump.» What does jelly mean here? | A sweet spread for toast | A jellyfish | *Soft and wobbly | Weak and shaky
exam: Jayden reads «a newborn kangaroo is about the size of a jelly bean» and says jelly means a dessert. Is he right? | No, jelly bean is a fruit | Yes, a jelly bean is a sweet spread | *No, jelly bean is a small sweet, and the clue is the size, not a dessert | Yes, a jelly bean is always a dessert
